      "id": "n40-lookup-method-comparison",
      "source_numbers": [
        "829"
      ],
      "view": {
        "mode": "single",
        "stem": "Evaluate $6\\sqrt{\\frac13}$ by two table routes: route 1 looks up $\\sqrt{0.3333}$ and multiplies the reported root by $6$; route 2 first rewrites the exact expression as $\\sqrt{12}$ and looks it up. Which option gives the complete computed comparison and identifies the route needing fewer table/correction/rescaling operations?\n\nFor these items, use the following transcribed Table IV cells from Bradis's Four-figure Mathematical Tables (2010). Decimal commas are written as decimal points. A row N below 10 uses main-column digit j for argument N+j/100 and correction-column digit k for the extra argument k/1000. A row N at least 10 uses j/10 and k/100 instead. Add 0.001 times the correction-cell integer to the main root entry. A dash means no correction is needed. Normalize any out-of-range positive argument as a*10^(2m), with 1 <= a < 100, and scale the table root by 10^m.\n\n| Row N | Main column j | Main root entry | Correction column k | Correction integer |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 12 | 0 | 3.464 | - | - |\n| 33 | 3 | 5.771 | 3 | 3 |\n\nCompute both routes using these cells. Keep the first route's product before any final rounding; then compare both reports at three decimal places. Judge convenience by the number of required table/correction/rescaling operations.",
        "figure_ids": [],
        "options": [
          {
            "id": "a",
            "text": "Route 1: $3.4626$ before final rounding; route 2: $3.464$. Their three-decimal reports are $(3.463,\\ 3.464)$; route 2 needs fewer operations.",
            "figure_ids": []
          },
          {
            "id": "b",
            "text": "Route 1: $3.4644$ before final rounding; route 2: $3.464$. Their three-decimal reports are $(3.464,\\ 3.464)$; route 2 needs fewer operations.",
            "figure_ids": []
          },
          {
            "id": "c",
            "text": "Route 1: $0.34644$ before final rounding; route 2: $3.464$. Their three-decimal reports are $(0.346,\\ 3.464)$; route 2 needs fewer operations.",
            "figure_ids": []
          },
          {
            "id": "d",
            "text": "Route 1: $3.4644$ before final rounding; route 2: $3.464$. Their three-decimal reports are $(3.464,\\ 3.464)$; route 1 needs fewer operations.",
            "figure_ids": []
          }
        ]
      }
