        "830"
      ],
      "view": {
        "mode": "single",
        "stem": "Use the square-root table to evaluate $4\\sqrt5$, $15\\sqrt2$, $14\\sqrt{\\frac17}$ and $12\\sqrt{\\frac56}$, in that order. Which tuple gives all four reported numerical values after the required exact preparation and root rescaling?\n\nFor these items, use the following transcribed Table IV cells from Bradis's Four-figure Mathematical Tables (2010). Decimal commas are written as decimal points. A row N below 10 uses main-column digit j for argument N+j/100 and correction-column digit k for the extra argument k/1000. A row N at least 10 uses j/10 and k/100 instead. Add 0.001 times the correction-cell integer to the main root entry. A dash means no correction is needed. Normalize any out-of-range positive argument as a*10^(2m), with 1 <= a < 100, and scale the table root by 10^m.\n\n| Row N | Main column j | Main root entry |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 1.2 | 0 | 1.095 |\n| 4.5 | 0 | 2.121 |\n| 28 | 0 | 5.292 |\n| 80 | 0 | 8.944 |\n\nFirst absorb each positive outside coefficient into its square root, then normalize the radicand to 1 <= a < 100. Use these main entries and report the scaled products without further rounding.",
        "figure_ids": [],
        "options": [
          {
            "id": "a",
            "text": "$(8.944,\\ 2.121,\\ 5.292,\\ 1.095)$",
            "figure_ids": []
